Conditional welfare programmes create poverty traps: recipients who find part-time work often lose more in benefits than they gain in wages; UBI's unconditional design eliminates this disincentive completely.
A 2019 meta-analysis of 31 cash transfer programmes across 26 countries found no consistent evidence of reduced work effort — the 'welfare dependency' hypothesis is not supported by the data.
Consumer spending by low-income households has a multiplier effect of 1.5–2× in local economies, meaning UBI spending circulates through communities rather than accumulating in savings.
Education outcomes improve in families receiving unconditional cash: the PROGRESA programme in Mexico (now Oportunidades) increased secondary school completion by 23% among participating families.
